// PARAM: --analysis interval
// Both branches of the if end in a call to the same procedure, so both calls
// resume at the same CFG node: the compiler emits pp4 --call-to-return f--> pp6
// and pp5 --call-to-return f--> pp6. That node's equation therefore carries two
// call actions to f, both routing to the same context, so one right-hand-side
// evaluation performs two side effects to the same callee-entry seed key.
//
// The vendored solver applies its per-origin update eagerly per side effect,
// with the running accumulate rather than the completed one, and
// update_global_per_origin overwrites the origin's slot. The dead branch's
// write lands first carrying bottom, dropping the seed below what the live
// branch established, which destabilizes the callee and re-solves it at
// bottom; the live write restores it and destabilizes again, forever. The
// livelock is a fixed 10-step cycle over 11 distinct solver states, not an
// ascending chain -- the lattice this program reaches is finite.
//
// Reversing the branch polarity hides it: the live write then lands first and
// the running accumulate never dips. So this case pins the polarity that
// exposes the defect, and pins it under --context none, where the default
// Interval configuration hits it.
void f(n) {
  return n
}
void main() {
  x := 5;
  if (x < 2) {
    a := f(1)
  } else {
    a := f(1)
  };
  __voblint_check(x == 5) // PROVED
}
